{
    "summary": "c0.m0() precision limited by machine epsilon"
    "description": "Similar to the issue described in [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MATH-201 \"T-test p-value precision hampered by machine epsilon\"), using c0.m0() with many treatments results in p-values that are continuous down to 2.2e-16 but that drop to 0 after that.\nIn [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MATH-201 \"T-test p-value precision hampered by machine epsilon\"), the problem was described as such:  \n> So in essence, the p-value returned by TTestImpl.tTest() is:  \n>   \n> 1.0 - (cumulativeProbability(t) - cumulativeProbabily(-t))  \n>   \n> For large-ish t-statistics, cumulativeProbabilty(-t) can get quite small, and cumulativeProbabilty(t) can get very close to 1.0. When   \n> cumulativeProbability(-t) is less than the machine epsilon, we get p-values equal to zero because:  \n>   \n> 1.0 - 1.0 + 0.0 = 0.0\nThe solution in [](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MATH-201 \"T-test p-value precision hampered by machine epsilon\") was to modify the p-value calculation to this:  \n> p = 2.0 \\* cumulativeProbability(-t)\nHere, the problem is similar. From c0.m0():  \n p = 2 \\* (1 - tDistribution.cumulativeProbability(t));\nDirectly calculating the p-value using identical code as c0.m0(), but with the following change seems to solve the problem:  \n p = 2 \\* (tDistribution.cumulativeProbability(-t));\n"
}

